Heh!  That is probably the single oldest prohibition on appropriate
use of computer systems in existence.  I chuckle because I remember
seeing much the same thing on mainframes at graduate school in
the early 1970s and at work shortly thereafter.

Game playing would increase, sysadmins would notice resources being
sucked up accordingly, prohibitions would occur, some people would
quit playing and some would not, sysadmins would erase games or make
their directories inaccessible, gameplaying would further decline,
programmers and users would install new copies of games, game playing
would increase...

It is a never-ending cycle.  And yes, the sysadmins (the university,
in this instance) are within their rights and perogatives to do this.
How appropriate doing so is rather depends on the specific situation
at the system you mention.


