I don't know about other universities, but here at UTSA, the following
conditions apply:

1. PC Software Support: 2 full-time technicians, 5 work-study
2. PC Hardware Support: 3 full-time technicians, 9 work-study
3. Main Student PC lab: 1 full-time technician , 6 work-study

I don't know about the mainframe room, but I would not be surprised if
similar conditions applied.

Note that the above figures are for a University with over 2500 faculty
and staff, and something like 23,000 registered students. Each full-time
faculty/staffer has a PC of some description on her/his desk, plus
additional PCs in classrooms, labs and research areas. For example,
I have seven PCs in my Center, and two of my PhDs have PCs in their
separated (non-Center) offices.

Part of the problems you mentioned may be due to inadequate training of
computing center personnel. I do not have simular problems here for one
simple reason -- I have more communications-electronics-computer
experience than just about _all_ of the PC support sections combined, and
they know it. I was a military comm-elec type for over ten years.

When I get the kind of response that you describe getting from your
computer people, I simply get on my phone and call the Director of
Information Technology. This usually clears up any problems. Have you tried
something similar?

