  Not policy at all, but I've had multiple similar situations.

  If it's just posting and it's the appropriate group for the topic (as
defined by the guidelines, etc) I don't think you have much of a leg to
stand on.  If they crosspost, however, it greatly increases their chance
of posting something somewhere where it doesn't belong.  At this point
you might consider offering them "help" on how to post "properly."  (:

  IMHO, irrelevant is far worse than inflamatory on the net.  At least
you can do something about it...  telling someone that political postings
have no bearing on, say, recreational bike riding is something clear-cut.

  Having an excuse to take a little personal interest in the human that's
responsible always helps in my book.  Sending them mail from a local mail-
box doesn't always help.  They sometimes think they're faceless, untouchable
and anonymous.  Going up to them and dropping a big printout of complaints
on their lap and asking them which part of Emily Postnews they didn't
understand is another.  (:  <it certainly didn't hurt in my case>

  Be sure you try to see things from the local user's perspective, too.
I had one case where it was the guy on the other side that was really
the rude/crude type, although he wrote real sweet messages to me complaining
about the local user.  If you can, try to decide for yourself if the
user is within their rights to be behaving like they are before you
approach them.

