>>What do you do?

Having gone through an identical scenario recently, I'm proud to say that
we (in the form of the Computing Centre, that is) did nothing.  For
several reasons:

a - being an academic institution in a divided society, the question of
    censorship raises its ugly head.  Are we advocates of free speech
    (no matter how unpleasant) or of some sort of political
    correctness?  Once you stamp on a particular user for posting stuff
    that certain individuals find objectionable, where do you draw the
    line?  Can you ever again defend your site against the accusation
    that you're restricting what your users can say?  This works both
    ways, with a vengeance...

b - it turned out that the offending user was undergoing some sort of
    unsuccessful treatment for a personality disorder.  His somewhat
    incoherent but highly libellous and widely cross posted gems were
    eventually drawn to the attention of the psychiatrist treating him
    (by members of his class who were in a position to know something
    about his background), resulting in renewed therapy.  There's no
    way that I, as a sysadmin, can check on, or make judgments about,
    the sanity or otherwise of anyone posting to Usenet from our site.
    I can suspect it, sure, but I'm very unclear on the ethics of
    actually acting on my suspicions, for the same reasons I have no
    desire to dabble with censorship.

>You might send complainers a tutorial on how to use killfiles.  

This was done in our case...  together with the suggestion that
it isn't mandatory to read everything on Usenet, let alone take it
seriously.

The point was also made that there is an implicit disclaimer with every
posting to Usenet - there's no way that users at a particular site can
claim to be propagating some sort of official point of view of the
organisation running the site.  The nature of the medium is such that
forgeries can be made, and experienced users are well aware of this.

Unfortunately, complainants in such cases are usually newcomers to
Usenet and its little ways :-(

>      State-supported academic institutions are bound by their laws and
>regulations, so there legal advice may be appropriate.

In our case, action can only be taken against staff or students if a
formal complaint is made to the Dean of Students and or the
disciplinary committee.  No formal complaints were forthcoming.

>      It's worth reminding users who send out dumb postings that all
>postings are archived, indexed, and sold on CD-ROMs, and some employers
>do check up on prospective employees that way.

This was also tried, to no visible effect.  Understandably, in
retrospect.
