In article <1993Feb21.051627.3876@selway.umt.edu> cs000rjp@selway.umt.edu (Russell J. Pagenkopf) writes:
> [ Situation: user posts long, irrelevant, possibly inflammatory stuff
    to newsgroups. The author (and sysadmin) receives mail from someone
    complaining about the user's posts. ]

>What do you do?

In this case, it matters what kind of setting you're in.

1. Educational setting: most universities have a computing policy of
   some sort. Unless the user is doing something like posting material
   to alt.sex.* when the policy forbids posting of this kind of thing,
   there's probably not much you can do. Most universities also have
   some kind of freedom of expression act. Sending the user some polite
   mail on etiquette might be a good idea, though.

2. Corporate setting: talking to the user's supervisor/manager might
   be a good idea. Of course, most people also post with the disclaimer,
   "I speak only for myself,"  but a reprimand from The Boss might
   do some good.

3. Commercial access: (as far as I know) not much you can do other
   than sending mail on etiquette.

At least a quarter of the material posted on USENET is probably
flamebait, or responses to flamebait.  :)   This is why killfiles
exist. There are an awful lot of people out there with odd opinions
and forceful ways of expressing them. One of the nice things about
electronic communication is that you can simply ignore postings 
that you don't want to read.

Most people eventually get tired of being flamed, if they're just
posting inflammatory material for kicks. IMHO, unless the individual
is posting using company access, the system administrator is under
no obligation to control what the user posts, unless he's posting
illegal material.

