I have set up a Gopher service called Healthline which includes a Link
to Veronica (a program that searches known Gopher menu items for keywords
given by the user).  Presently, access to Healthline is public to those
who know how to get in (i.e. via dial-up lines), but that is subject to
change at any moment (especially after "they" see this posting ;-)! 
Healthline is run on a University of Montana DECStation 5500, and users who
know Veronica well enough can use it to search for abpe materials even
though they are screened out from the local news feed.

The intended purpose of Healthline is to disseminate health-related local
and internet-wide information, and the purpose of Veronica is to allow
users to search for *health-related* topics which aren't covered in
Healthline.  But the nightmare I see is 16 year old Johnnie Jones
logging into Healthline, using veronica to search for appropriate
materials, and downloading files to his hearts content.  Then mom
comes along and says "Where did you get those!!!????" And the University
ends up with a nice costly lawsuit.

The University could argue that the user has to go to great lengths to
uudecode/piece together/view apbe postings and thus "knowingly" abused
the intended resources of Healthline, but I don't know if that would be 
enough.  Comments?
