I'm sorry if the references I provided seemed unrelated to the
scenerio presented. I believe they were relevant.

Recall the scenerio involved a university-provided Network service,
Healthline, that included a Veronica server. The Veronica server
provides access to many gopher servers at other sites some of which
provide access to netnews. The Netnews servers provide access to many
newsgroups each of which includes many netnews articles originally
posted from many other sites. Johnnie's access some of those articles
may upset his mom. Or as jdc@selway.umt.edu (John-David Childs) wrote:
'Then mom comes along and says "Where did you get those!!!????" And
the University ends up with a nice costly lawsuit.'

I started by response: "Sued on what grounds?". I ended my response
with references to four files:
 
I referenced "ftp.eff.org:pub/academic/faq/netnews.liability", a file
that documents the claim that since the university was acting like a
bookstore or library and not a publisher, it seems unlikely that it
would be held liable for access to the material.

I referenced "ftp.eff.org:pub/academic/faq/netnews.reading", a file
that argues and documents the proposition that a university *should
not* restrict material just because it is offensive to some.

I referenced "ftp.eff.org:pub/academic/faq/censorship-and-harassment",
a file that documents the claim that a university is not legally
*required* to restrict material just because it is offensive to some.

Finally, I included a brief blurb about the Computers and Academic
Freedom mailing lists, since the scenerio seemed to fit CAF.

I believe it important to back up one's words with references. I
believe the specific references I provided in the article were
relevant.

For those who don't care to read or check references, I try to make
them easy to skip. They are always the last part of an article; they
always start "ANNOTATED REFERENCES".

- Carl
