     All this sort of thing is beside the point.  Another person has
already posted the sole salient issue, namely, that the gopher server
in question merely provides information as to the location and/or
method of connecting to the games and has nothing to do with a) storage
or execution of, or other resources consumed by playing, the games in
question.  The gopher server is merely providing the information in the
same manner as it provides any other information.  A gopher server is,
in essence, like any other directory information server (e.g. a webster
server, a U. of I. CSO phone directory server, a WAIS server, an on-line
encyclopaedia server, an OCLC server, etc.)
     If an institution doesn't want people playing games over the net,
then it should take effective action to prevent people from *accessing*
games over the net.  Simply making the information on *how* to access
such games unavailable is essentially the "security through obscurity"
trip, which is quite thoroughly ineffective.  Expecting a different,
autonomous institution to restrict the information is simply ludicrous
on the face of it.
     There is no justification in a free society for the sort of censor-
ship of publicly available information that rwwatt@watserv1.uwaterloo.ca
implies should be practiced.  It is somewhat appalling to observe such 
statements issuing from a renowned academic institution.


