I don't think the previous poster was discussing copyright issues at all,
but rather the general handling of a newsgroup dedicated to erotic pictures.


>A university has a "continuing education
>center" in Portland that was nailed for a $50,000 fine <plus> other
>costs by the Software Publishers Association.  The problem with abpe is
>not just that it may violate local pornography laws (the idea of showing
>up on the local news shows during sweeps week as "Portland's Porn King"
>doesn't have much appeal to me ...), but that the material is illegally
>copied, just as if it were copies of WordPerfect or UNIX.  But I'm not a 
>lawyer.  Presumably your educational institution has one on staff somewhere.  
>Ask them about your particular situation.

You make a fine point, and it is one that is discussed regularly with
regard to the large volume of copyrighted material that gets posted to
abpe (and probably other picture groups too).

But your analogy to software piracy isn't too close.  For a close analogy,
we need to look at the following questions:

1) What would be the university's liability be if a single piece of copyrighted
software were illegally posted to a "binaries" or "sources" newsgroup that
the university carried?

2) What would the university's liability be if copyrighted software were
occasionally (regularly, but in very low volume) illegally posted to a
"binaries" or "sources" newsgroup that the university carried?

3) What would the university's liability be if copyrighted software were
frequently (in large volume) illegally posted to a "binaries" or "sources"
newsgroup that the university carried?


Assuming that the university does not monitor/censor it newsfeed, in general,
my guess is that the answer to questions 1 and 2 are certainly "The
university has no liability."

Furthermore, my guess is that the answer to 3 must be the same.  Why?
Simply this--if the case of 3 were allowed to establish liability for
the university, then any group of "thugs" could force any newsgroup
(at least, any unmoderated newsgroup) to become a liability to the
university (simply by posting illegal material), and the university would
be forced to drop it.

In short, it would deny the university, and its news-reading/posting members,
the right to use the group for *lawful* exchanges.


[...]

>The fact is, they <do> make laws against pornography.

Who are "they" and what laws have "they" made against pornography?  Last
time I checked, non-obscene pornography was constitutionally protected.
(And it was very rare for a court to deem something "obscene.")


>And most of this
>seems to be illegal from a copyright standpoint anyway.

Illegal for the poster, certainly.   Probably illegal for one
who retrieves a copyrighted picture and keeps it (knowingly violating
the copyright, that is).

That the NetNews provider has done anything illegal is seriously
questionable, however.

And of course, this has nothing to do with pornography, as you point out.


