In article <1993Feb25.204011.5379@craycos.com> jrbd@craycos.com (James Davies) writes:

>Another thought: isn't this also in the spirit of the CERT advisory
>on monitoring remote logins that caused such a stink a few months ago?
>(It was noised about as "DOD authorizes keystroke monitoring", but it
>was really advising computer managers to make advance notice of the
>possibility of monitoring to avoid legal problems if an intruder was detected.)

The more I reflect on that CERT advisory, the more I have trouble with
it. 

Apparently, the folks at CERT got their information about the relevant
law (the Electronic Communications Privacy Act) from the Department of
Justice. They were told that ECPA is "ambiguous" as to whether the
provider of an information service (such providers range from 
RBOCs to Usenet sites to local BBSs) could capture the keystrokes
of a computer intruder.

But when I look at ECPA, I find no ambiguity at all. 18 USC 2702(b)(5)
and 18 USC 2702(b)(6) allow a provider to divulge stored communications
when doing so is incident to protection of the system, or when the
provider comes across crime-related communications inadvertently. And
18 USC 2511(2)(a)(i) has similar provisions for interceptions of
communications.

In trying to figure out why the government might advise
government-computer operators to post public notices informing users about
monitoring, I realized that such warnings have an important secondary
effect--they eliminate any claim *any* user (authorized or unauthorized)
might have regarding an expectation of privacy on the system. Users
who have seen the warning would have no "reasonable expectation of
privacy" at all--ergo, capturing their communications or reading their
files would not violate either ECPA or the Fourth Amendment.

Not having to seek a warrant certainly does make law-enforcement's job
easier.



--Mike




