From: NEELY_MP@DARWIN.NTU.EDU.AU (Mark P. Neely, Northern Territory University)
Subject: Liability of Shareware Authors
Message-ID: <930227085332.3404@DARWIN.NTU.EDU.AU>
Date: Sat, 27 Feb 1993 08:53:32 GMT

>Should the author of shareware be held liable for possible damages caused by
>the use of their product, and if so to what extent?  

I imagine the answer depends on whether the user has paid for the right to use
the software in question (i.e. has performed a trial run & decided to pay for a
licence). If that is the case, then one could imply a term into the contract
between the author + user (for which consideration has been paid) that the
software would be reasonably fit for the use for which it was purchased (e.g.
if your pd wordprocessor formats the hard-disk:- el breacho!).

Whilst I haven't given it much thought, there would appear to be little chance
of a "contractual" remedy for bug-ridden pd software if the user has paid _no_
money for the right to use - there would be no consideration to support the
finding of a contract between the two parties.

However, a tortious remedy may be available - negligent software design.

(That'd be an interesting one.)

