>resources, that your organization should have no policy on the acceptable 
>uses of its resources? Does the governing body of your organization have that 
>same view?

     If you still have it available, please reread the rest of my article.
Now, perhaps my wording was not clear, so I'll attempt to be more direct
here.
     A gopher server simply provides information to whoever requests it.
The requestor could be a typical gopher client program, but it certainly
does not need to be.  For example, a telnet client can be used, but is
quite inconvenient and the information returned from the server queried
is not very pretty to read.  What the recipient human does with the
information is not known to the server.
     A gopher server does nothing else.  It does not provide login
sessions, gaming sessions, IRC conversations, phone directory connections,
etc.  
     A system administrator stated that he had refused to suppress the
dissemination of certain information (N.B. *not* provision of gaming or
other interactive sessions) by his server.  You called that irresponsible.
I called your calling his refusal irresponsible an appalling statement
to be issuing from an academic institution.
     If that system administrator had agreed to suppress the information 
in response to the complaint from someone at another organization, that
system administrator would indeed have been practicing censorship.  And
*that* is what I claim would be inappropriate in a free society.
     You appear to have muddled the use of "free".  My use of "free"
refered to liberty and to basic rights that confer and allow the
preservation of liberty.  Your use of "free" refers to prices for goods
and/or services.
     Now, to answer your questions:  1) Yes, I happen mostly to agree
that users should be held accountable for their use of public resources,
though what they do with their own resources is, of course, nobody's
concern but their own.  In fact, however, many institutions have an
explicit policy that does not encourage such accountability, e.g. our
university charges users for certain resources associated with use of
the campus mainframe, but not for other resources.  Further, because
the computer center is allocated a more-or-less fixed budget, once
that budget amount has been recovered through chargebacks, the computer
center is not allowed to charge users for using those resources for
the remainder of the fiscal year.  2) An "acceptable use" policy is
a very good thing to have.  Dissemination of information doesn't violate
any policy on acceptable use that I ever heard of, except when such
dissemination would violate some contract associated with the data, e.g.
proprietary software.


